      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment carefully.\n\nThis is an OIML Certificate R49/2013-A-NL1-19.01 revision 6 for electromagnetic water meters.\n\nKey data:\n- Certificate number: R49/2013-A-NL1-19.01 revision 6\n- Scheme: A\n- Project number: 4086814\n- Page total: 7\n- Member state: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 10 August 2026 (from the \"Issuing Authority NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1, 10 August 2026\" part)\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1\n\nIssuing authority:\n- Name: NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\n- Address: Thijseweg 11, 2629 JA Delft, the Netherlands\n- Person responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\n- Phone: +31 88 636 2332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n\nApplicants:\n- Euromag International S.r.l., Via Balla, 55, 35010 Villafranca Padovana (PD), Italy\n\nManufacturers:\n- Same as applicant: Euromag International S.r.l. (labeled \"Applicant and Manufacturer\")\n\nCertified type:\n- Category: \"Water meter\" (electromagnetic water meter)\n- Type designations: MUT1000EL, MUT2200EL, MUT2300 (measuring sensor types) with electronic converter MC406M and MC406AM\n\nRecommendation:\n- R49-1 (2013) → canonical id \"R49\", edition 2013? Hmm. The recommendation is \"R49-1 (2013)\" \"Water meters intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot water\". The R49 recommendation has parts. The schema says id (canonical, e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\"), edition (int). R49-1 is part 1. Edition 2013. Hmm, should id be \"R49\" or \"R49-1\"? The rule says canonical e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". I'll use \"R49\" for id and maybe note part... The schema doesn't have a \"part\" field. I could use id \"R49-1\"? The instruction says canonical id like \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\" — it's about removing the \"OIML\" prefix. I think \"R49-1\" preserves the part information. Actually, let me think. R49 consists of parts 1, 2, 3. The certificate says \"R49-1 (2013)\". I'll use id \"R49-1\" and edition 2013. Hmm, but the canonical form example suggests \"R60\". Maybe I should use \"R49\" and add... there's no place for part. I'll go with \"R49-1\" to be faithful. Actually wait — accuracy classes: \"1 and 2\".\n\nAccuracy classes: [1, 2]? Accuracy class strings — the certificate says \"Accuracy class 1 and 2\". Also Table 1: Accuracy class of MUT2200EL = 2, MUT2300 = 1.\n\nTest reports: from page 2:\n- No. NMi-15200444-01 dated 31 March 2016 that includes 39 pages\n- NMi-16200309-01 revision 1 dated 22 March 2018 that includes 29 pages\n- No. NMi-1902198-01 dated 3 December 2018 that includes 16 pages\n- No. NMi-2186686-01 dated 24 May 2019 that includes 43 pages\n- No. NMi-2186686-02 dated 24 May 2019 that includes 47 pages\n- No. NMi-2463352-01 dated 18 January 2021 that includes 27 pages\n- No. NMi-3369067-01 dated 10 May 2022 that includes 13 pages\n- No. NMi-3670720-01 dated 22 September 2023 that includes 12 pages\n- No. NMi-4086814-01 dated 10 August 2026 that includes 21 pages\n\nRoles: These are \"associated reports\" — likely type evaluation reports. The schema role options: type_evaluation_report | test_report | documentation_file | evaluation_report. Hmm. The text says \"The conformity was established by the results of tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\". These are reports associated with the certificate — most are type evaluation reports presumably. But without more info, I'd mark them as \"test_report\" or \"type_evaluation_report\". The certificate mentions \"the OIML Type Evaluation Report\" (singular, referenced in the intro). These listed reports include tests and examinations. I'll mark them as \"test_report\" generally? Hmm. Actually, in OIML-CS, the associated reports to a certificate are the Type Evaluation Reports. But the listed ones look like test reports that accumulated over revisions. Given ambiguity, I'll use \"test_report\" for all — hmm, but \"the associated OIML Type Evaluation Report(s)\" is mentioned. The safest: role \"type_evaluation_report\" for all since they're \"associated reports\" supporting the type evaluation. Actually let me reconsider: the certificate says \"This Certificate attests the conformity of the above identified type (represented by the sample(s) identified in the OIML Type Evaluation Report)\". The list on page 2: \"The conformity was established by the results of tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\". These reports are test/evaluation reports. I'll assign \"test_report\" to each. Hmm, both defensible. I'll go with \"test_report\".\n\nAlso documentation folder: \"The construction of the measuring instrument is recorded in the Documentation folder no. T10713-10.\" → test_reports entry with id \"T10713-10\", role \"documentation_file\".\n\nModel family: The cert covers multiple types: MUT1000EL, MUT2200EL, MUT2300. Plus converters MC406M, MC406AM.\n\nThe models: MUT1000EL (full bore, without flanges), MUT2200EL (full bore, with flanges), MUT2300 (reduced bore). Also converter variants.\n\nCharacteristics from Table 1:\n- Measuring principle: Electromagnetic (type level, string value)\n- Accuracy class of MUT2200EL: 2 (model level)\n- Accuracy class of MUT2300: 1 (model level)\n- Environmental class: M1 / O (installed outdoors) — string\n- Electromagnetic environment: varies by converter version — config level? This is per converter version: E1 for remote MC406M; E2 for compact MC406M; E2 for compact and remote MC406AM; E1 for compact and remote MC406M/AM with Bluetooth&RS485 and/or 4-20mA output. This is config-level with axis \"converter configuration\".\n- Temperature range ambient: -25°C / +55°C → {min: -25, max: 55}, unit °C\n- Water temperature class: T50 (+0,1 °C / +50 °C) → water_temperature_class: \"T50\", with range {min: 0.1, max: 50} °C. Note the decimal comma: +0,1 → 0.1.\n- Maximum admissible pressure (MAP): 1,6 MPa (16 bar) → value 1.6, unit MPa; also secondary 16 bar. I could record map_mpa = 1.6 MPa and map_bar = 16 bar? Or value 1.6 MPa. I'll record maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa and maybe separate map_alternative: 16 bar. Simpler: one characteristic \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" with value 1.6, unit MPa. Or include both in matrix_tables? I'll add a second characteristic maximum_admissible_pressure_bar: 16, unit bar. Hmm, that might be noise but it's accurate. I'll do both: maximum_admissible_pressure (1.6 MPa) and maximum_admissible_pressure_bar (16 bar)? Actually the parenthetical is just a conversion. I'll keep just the primary and note. Let me include both to be safe but name the second clearly. Actually rules say don't fabricate but this is present. I'll include \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" value 1.6 MPa, and separate \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" 16 bar isn't great. Alternative: use matrix_tables for Table 1? Table 1 is a general characteristics key-value table. Better as characteristics.\n\n- Orientation: All positions (Horizontal, vertical or diagonal) — string\n- Flow profile sensitivity class: U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream) — string\n- Reverse flow: designed to measure reverse flow — string/boolean true\n\nPage 3 table (continuation of general characteristics):\n- Pressure loss class of full-bore type MUT1000EL or MUT2200EL: Δp 10 for all sizes (based on documentation 10713/6-01, 10713/6-02, 10713/12-01)\n- Pressure loss class of reduced bore type MUT2300: Δp 25 for sizes < DN80; Δp 40 for sizes ≥ DN80 (based on documentation 10713/1-01)\n- Pressure loss class of reduced bore type MUT2300 (based on doc 10713/4-03, 10713/12-02, 10713/12-03): For sizes < DN250 see table; For sizes ≥ DN250: Δp 40 (0,040 MPa or 0,40 bar)\n\nNested table: Meter size / Pressure loss class / Alternative pressure loss class:\n- DN50: Δp 25 | Δp 10 for Q3 ≤ 25 m³/h\n- DN65: Δp 25 | Δp 10 for Q3 ≤ 40 m³/h\n- DN80: Δp 40 | Δp 16 for Q3 ≤ 63 m³/h\n- DN100: Δp 40 | Δp 16 for Q3 ≤ 100 m³/h\n- DN125: Δp 16 | N/A\n- DN150: Δp 40 | Δp 16 for Q3 ≤ 250 m³/h\n- DN200: Δp 16 | N/A\n\nThis is a matrix table with model (size) as rows. Extract as matrix_tables AND model_level characteristics (pressure loss class by size). The sizes DN50...DN200 — are these the model family? The model family here is at type level (MUT1000EL, MUT2200EL, MUT2300). The DN sizes are configurations/sizes within. Hmm.\n\nFor model_family.models: The certificate covers types MUT1000EL, MUT2200EL, MUT2300 with converters MC406M, MC406AM. Should models be these types? Yes: models[] with model_id \"MUT1000EL\", \"MUT2200EL\", \"MUT2300\". Attributes: accuracy class (MUT2200EL=2, MUT2300=1), bore (full/reduced), pressure loss class.\n\nAccuracy class model_level:\n- attribute: accuracy_class, values: [{model: MUT2200EL, value: 2}, {model: MUT2300, value: 1}]. MUT1000EL accuracy class not given in Table 1 (interesting — only MUT2200EL and MUT2300 have accuracy classes listed). So omit MUT1000EL.\n\nPressure loss by size — the DN sizes could be model-level entries too. But model family already defined at type level. The DN sizes are meter sizes (configurations). I could add model_level entries with model like \"DN50\"? That would conflict. Better: put the size table in matrix_tables, and optionally add config_level entries for pressure_loss_class with axis \"meter_size\". Let me use config_level:\n\n- attribute: pressure_loss_class, axis: meter_size_bore_type, values:\n  Actually there are multiple bases (documentation). Let me structure:\n\nconfig_level entries:\n1. attribute: pressure_loss_class_full_bore, axis: size, values: [{condition: \"all sizes (MUT1000EL or MUT2200EL)\", value: \"Δp 10\"}]\n2. attribute: pressure_loss_class_reduced_bore_mut2300, axis: size, values: [{condition: \"sizes < DN80\", value: \"Δp 25\"}, {condition: \"sizes ≥ DN80\", value: \"Δp 40\"}]\n3. From the nested table: attribute: pressure_loss_class_mut2300, axis: meter_size, values per size with alternative class.\n\nActually the nested table itself is best captured in matrix_tables with name \"Pressure loss classes for MUT2300 (documentation 10713/4-03, 10713/12-02, 10713/12-03)\", columns: meter_size, pressure_loss_class, alternative_pressure_loss_class, alternative_q3_condition. Hmm, the alternative column combines class and condition: \"Δp 10 for Q3 ≤ 25 m³/h\". I can split: alternative_pressure_loss_class \"Δp 10\", alternative_condition \"Q3 ≤ 25 m³/h\". That's cleaner. Rows:\n\n- DN50: Δp 25, alt Δp 10 (Q3 ≤ 25 m³/h)\n- DN65: Δp 25, alt Δp 10 (Q3 ≤ 40 m³/h)\n- DN80: Δp 40, alt Δp 16 (Q3 ≤ 63 m³/h)\n- DN100: Δp 40, alt Δp 16 (Q3 ≤ 100 m³/h)\n- DN125: Δp 16, alt N/A\n- DN150: Δp 40, alt Δp 16 (Q3 ≤ 250 m³/h)\n- DN200: Δp 16, alt N/A\n\n- Power supply: Replaceable battery (2,9 – 3,7 V); DC mains (10 - 28 V) only for MC406AM.\n  → characteristics:\n  - power_supply_battery: value \"replaceable battery\" (string) — or power_supply_type.\n  - battery_voltage: {min: 2.9, max: 3.7}, unit V\n  - power_supply_dc_mains_voltage: {min: 10, max: 28}, unit V, condition: only for MC406AM → could be config_level with axis converter. Since it applies only to MC406AM, config_level: attribute dc_mains_supply_voltage, axis: converter, values: [{condition: \"MC406AM only\", value: {min:10, max:28}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}]. Or type-level with note. I'll do config_level.\n\n- Software identification: Bootloader software versions with CRC checksums:\n  - 01.00 → 63A2EDED\n  - 01.01 → 67AEA1E4\n  - 01.02 → DE7A99AB\n  - 01.02 → 0CC44DD4 (note: 01.02 appears twice with different checksums — keep as-is)\n  \n  This could be a matrix_table \"Bootloader software versions and CRC checksums\" with columns software_version, crc_checksum, component (\"Bootloader\"). I'll add as matrix_table.\n\nDate issued: 10 August 2026 (date next to the Issuing Authority signature block). Format as ISO: \"2026-08-10\".\n\nRevision history: revision \"6\", date 2026-08-10? The revision number is 6 and the date issued is 10 August 2026. The revision 6 corresponds to this certificate issue. The report NMi-4086814-01 dated 10 August 2026 supports it. I'll add revision_history: [{revision: \"6\", date: \"2026-08-10\", changes: null}] — changes not stated, omit changes.\n\nCertificate number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-19.01\" with revision 6. The number field — should I include revision? Schema: number, scheme (A|B), project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id. So number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-19.01\", revision goes in revision_history. scheme: \"A\", project_number: \"4086814\", page_total: 7, member_state: \"The Netherlands\", date_issued: \"2026-08-10\", oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\".\n\nRecommendation: id \"R49-1\"? canonical e.g. \"R60\". I think \"R49\" with a note... schema has no part field. Hmm. Options: id \"R49-1\" preserves part. The example says id (canonical, e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\"). The point is stripping \"OIML\" prefix. R49-1 is the part designation. I'll use \"R49-1\". Hmm, but maybe safer \"R49\"? The certificate title number also starts \"R49/2013-A-...\". The recommendation is R49-1 (2013). I'll go with id: \"R49-1\", edition: 2013, amendment: null (omit), scheme: \"A\", accuracy_classes: [1, 2] — as strings or numbers? Accuracy classes \"1 and 2\". I'll use [\"1\", \"2\"] or [1, 2]. For R49, classes are usually \"1\" and \"2\" strings but numbers fine. I'll use strings? The schema example doesn't specify. I'll use [\"1\",\"2\"]... Actually numbers are cleaner: [1, 2]. Hmm, accuracy class in water meters is often denoted with vertical/horizontal too, but here just 1 and 2. Use [1, 2].\n\nWait — should recommendation accuracy_classes reflect both classes 1 and 2 overall. Yes.\n\nComponents: The certified type: \"An electromagnetic water meter, Type: MUT1000EL, MUT2200EL and MUT2300 with electronic converter MC406M and MC406AM\". The measuring sensor (MUT...) with electronic converter (MC406...). So components:\n- role: \"measuring sensor\" (the electromagnetic measuring sensor / meter body), type_designations: [\"MUT1000EL\", \"MUT2200EL\", \"MUT2300\"]\n- role: \"electronic converter\" (calculator/indicator?), type_designations: [\"MC406M\", \"MC406AM\"]\n\nThe electromagnetic environment characteristic varies by converter configuration — that's config_level with axis \"converter_version/configuration\":\n- E1: remote version of MC406M\n- E2: compact version of MC406M\n- E2: compact and remote version of MC406AM\n- E1: compact and remote version of MC406M/AM with Bluetooth&RS485 and/or 4-20mA output\n\nI'll model as config_level: attribute: electromagnetic_environment, axis: converter_configuration, values: [{condition: \"MC406M remote version\", value: \"E1\"}, {condition: \"MC406M compact version\", value: \"E2\"}, {condition: \"MC406AM compact or remote version\", value: \"E2\"}, {condition: \"MC406M or MC406AM compact or remote, with Bluetooth & RS485 and/or 4-20 mA output\", value: \"E1\"}].\n\nCategory: \"Water meter\" (category list includes... \"Gas meter\", etc. — \"Water meter\" fits).\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"MUT1000EL\", \"MUT2200EL\", \"MUT2300\", \"MC406M\", \"MC406AM\"]\n- module_designation: null — hmm, the R49 uses \"measuring instrument\" concepts. Module designation field is for load cells etc. I'd omit or null. The cert describes \"An electromagnetic water meter with electronic converter\". There's no \"module designation\" like \"Analog load cell\". I'll omit (not fabricate). Or null. I'll omit.\n- description: \"An electromagnetic water meter, type MUT1000EL, MUT2200EL and MUT2300, with electronic converter MC406M and MC406AM. The measurement sensor can have a full bore cylindrical measuring tube (MUT1000EL without flanges, MUT2200EL with flanges) or reduced bore (MUT2300).\"\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: null — hmm, is there an explicit family name? \"Table 2 and 3 gives an overview of the general characteristics of the family of instruments.\" No explicit family name. null.\n- models: [{model_id: \"MUT1000EL\", label: \"Full bore, without flanges\", attributes: {...}}, {model_id: \"MUT2200EL\", label: \"Full bore, with flanges\", attributes: {accuracy_class: 2}}, {model_id: \"MUT2300\", label: \"Reduced bore\", attributes: {accuracy_class: 1}}]\n\nattributes per model: MUT1000EL: bore \"full\", flanges false; accuracy unknown. MUT2200EL: accuracy_class 2, bore full, flanges true, pressure_loss_class \"Δp 10\". MUT2300: accuracy_class 1, bore reduced, pressure_loss_class varies by size.\n\nAlso converters are separate — components.\n\ncharacteristics.type_level:\n- measuring_principle: {value: \"Electromagnetic\", unit_symbol: null, unit_id: null}\n- environmental_class: {value: \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\"} — value string \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\". Keep string.\n- ambient_temperature_range: {value: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"} and water_temperature_range: {value: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}. The \"+0,1 °C / +50 °C\" — min 0.1, max 50. Note the original had comma decimal. Convert.\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"} — plus alternate bar: I'll add \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\": {value: 16, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}? Slightly redundant. Rules say pull unit out. The cell has both MPa and bar conversion. I'll include just MPa primary; but the 16 bar is extra info. I'll include a second characteristic maximum_admissible_pressure with value 1.6 MPa only. Hmm — but to preserve full fidelity, maybe include the bar as part of... I'll include both as separate characteristics: `maximum_admissible_pressure` (1.6 MPa) and `maximum_admissible_pressure_alternative_unit`... that's ugly. Simpler: value 1.6 MPa; the parenthetical (16 bar) is a unit conversion. Keep 1.6 MPa. Actually, I could add note. Schema doesn't have notes on type-level values. Keep just MPa.\n\nHmm wait, actually maybe I should keep both since \"don't fabricate\" but also \"preserve\". The bar value is genuinely there. I'll include two entries: \"maximum_admissible_pressure\": 1.6 MPa and \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\": 16 bar. Eh. I think including it is fine and preserves data. But naming: maybe \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" with value 1.6 unit MPa, and skip bar conversion as it's equivalent. The rules emphasize structured values; conversions are secondary. I'll skip the bar. Actually... let me include it as separate characteristic `maximum_admissible_pressure_bar` — no wait, that reads like pressure in bars applied... it's the same pressure. Fine, skip. Keep it clean.\n\n- orientation: {value: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\"}\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: {value: \"U0 and D0 (0 × DN upstream and 0 × DN downstream)\"} — keep as string. Avoid LaTeX — fine.\n- reverse_flow: {value: true} or string \"The water meter is designed to measure reverse flow\". I'll use boolean true? Structured value shape allows number|string. Boolean not listed... shape: number | {min,max} | string. So use string: \"designed to measure reverse flow\". OK.\n\nconfig_level entries:\n1. pressure_loss_class, axis \"bore_type / size (full-bore MUT1000EL or MUT2200EL)\", values: [{condition: \"all sizes\", value: \"Δp 10\"}] — hmm, config values shape: {condition, value}. Do they carry unit_symbol? Rule 5: \"Values follow the same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}.\" OK.\n\nLet me define:\n- attribute: pressure_loss_class, axis: \"size (full-bore type MUT1000EL / MUT2200EL)\", values: [{condition: \"all sizes\", value: \"Δp 10\"}], with maybe a note about basis documentation. The basis documentation is mentioned — \"based on documentation 10713/6-01, 10713/6-02 and 10713/12-01\". Where to put? Maybe in axis description or matrix_tables description. I'll create matrix_tables for these pressure loss items? They're mostly statements. Alternatively put documentation references into test_reports? No — they're documentation. Could add components? No.\n\nOption: matrix_tables entry \"Pressure loss classes\" with rows for each situation. But the primary structured nested table is the DN50-DN200 one. Let me do:\n\nmatrix_tables:\n1. name: \"Pressure loss classes for reduced-bore type MUT2300 (documentation 10713/4-03, 10713/12-02 and 10713/12-03)\", columns: [\"meter_size\", \"pressure_loss_class\", \"alternative_pressure_loss_class\", \"alternative_q3_limit\"], column_units: [null, null, null, \"m³/h\"], rows: 7 rows as above.\n2. name: \"Bootloader software versions\", columns: [\"component\", \"software_version\", \"crc_checksum\"], rows: 4 rows.\n\nAnd config_level:\n- pressure_loss_class_full_bore: axis \"size\": all sizes → Δp 10 (MUT1000EL/MUT2200EL).\n- pressure_loss_class_reduced_bore: axis \"size\": < DN80 → Δp 25; ≥ DN80 → Δp 40 (per documentation 10713/1-01); and ≥ DN250 → Δp 40 (per doc 10713/4-03 etc.).\n\nHmm, this gets complex. Maybe simpler: keep a config_level entry per documentation basis:\n\nconfig_level:\n[\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"pressure_loss_class\",\n    \"axis\": \"bore_type_and_size\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"full bore (MUT1000EL or MUT2200EL), all sizes (documentation 10713/6-01, 10713/6-02, 10713/12-01)\", \"value\": \"Δp 10\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"reduced bore (MUT2300), size < DN80 (documentation 10713/1-01)\", \"value\": \"Δp 25\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"reduced bore (MUT2300), size ≥ DN80 (documentation 10713/1-01)\", \"value\": \"Δp 40\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"reduced bore (MUT2300), size ≥ DN250 (documentation 10713/4-03, 10713/12-02, 10713/12-03)\", \"value\": \"Δp 40\"}\n    ]\n  }\n]\n\nAnd for sizes < DN250 of MUT2300, reference the nested table (matrix_tables).\n\nAlso electromagnetic_environment config_level as above.\n\nAlso DC mains config_level: attribute: dc_mains_supply_voltage, axis: converter, values: [{condition: \"MC406AM only\", value: {min: 10, max: 28}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}].\n\ntype_level power supply:\n- power_supply_type: {value: \"Replaceable battery\"} — hmm, battery voltage: battery_voltage_range {min: 2.9, max: 3.7} V.\n\nLet me structure:\n- power_supply: {value: \"Replaceable battery (2.9 – 3.7 V); DC mains (10–28 V) only for MC406AM\"}? No — better split:\n  - \"battery_power_supply\": {value: \"replaceable battery\"}\n  - \"battery_voltage\": {value: {min: 2.9, max: 3.7}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n  - config_level dc mains voltage.\n\nModel level characteristics:\n- accuracy_class: [{model: \"MUT2200EL\", value: 2}, {model: \"MUT2300\", value: 1}]\n- bore: [{model: \"MUT1000EL\", value: \"full bore\"}, {model: \"MUT2200EL\", value: \"full bore\"}, {model: \"MUT2300\", value: \"reduced bore\"}] — bore varies by model; it's more an attribute. Could be in model_family attributes. I'll put flange/bore info in model_family.models[].attributes and also possibly model_level. To avoid duplication, put bore in models attributes.\n\nmodels:\n- {model_id: \"MUT1000EL\", label: \"Full bore measuring tube, without flanges\", attributes: {\"bore\": \"full\", \"flanges\": false}}\n- {model_id: \"MUT2200EL\", label: \"Full bore measuring tube, with flanges\", attributes: {\"bore\": \"full\", \"flanges\": true, \"accuracy_class\": 2}}\n- {model_id: \"MUT2300\", label: \"Reduced bore measuring tube\", attributes: {\"bore\": \"reduced\", \"accuracy_class\": 1}}\n\nConverters — components with type_designations MC406M, MC406AM and attributes: available in compact and remote versions; with Bluetooth & RS485 and/or 4-20 mA output option. Components characteristics:\n\ncomponents:\n[\n  {\n    \"role\": \"measuring sensor (electromagnetic water meter primary element)\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"MUT1000EL\", \"MUT2200EL\", \"MUT2300\"],\n    \"characteristics\": {\n      \"config_level\": [...]? \n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"role\": \"electronic converter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"MC406M\", \"MC406AM\"],\n    ...\n  }\n]\n\nSchema for components: role, type_designations[], alternatives (\"OR\"|\"AND\"|null), characteristics{}. The characteristics{} free-form. For converter: available versions: compact, remote; MC406M and MC406AM — are these alternatives? The cert: \"with electronic converter MC406M and MC406AM\" — both used; each sensor type pairs with converters. alternatives: null (both included). I'd say alternatives null.\n\nMaybe keep components characteristics simple:\n- converter components characteristics: {\"versions\": [\"compact\", \"remote\"], \"options\": [\"Bluetooth & RS485\", \"4-20 mA output\"]}\n\nHmm, characteristics in components is a free object. But rule 2 says every characteristic value MUST be structured object. Let me keep component characteristics minimal or structured:\n\nFor measuring sensor:\n{\n  \"bore\": {\"value\": \"full bore (MUT1000EL without flanges, MUT2200EL with flanges) or reduced bore (MUT2300)\"}\n}\nEh. Maybe just omit characteristics in components since covered elsewhere. I'll include minimal.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: schema says components[]: role, type_designations[], alternatives, characteristics{}. I'll include characteristics where meaningful:\n\nsensor: characteristics: {\"cylindrical_measuring_tube\": {\"value\": \"full bore or reduced bore\"}} — eh. I'll include:\n- MUT sensor: {\"measuring_tube\": {\"value\": \"full bore (MUT1000EL without flanges; MUT2200EL with flanges) or reduced bore (MUT2300)\", \"unit_symbol\": null}}\n\nconverter: {\"versions\": ...} — keep as {\"available_versions\": {\"value\": \"compact and remote\"}}.\n\nNow footnotes: none in this fragment. revision_history: [{revision: \"6\", date: \"2026-08-10\"}]. changes: the cert doesn't state changes. Omit changes.\n\nTest reports (role): I'll mark all as \"type_evaluation_report\"? Or \"test_report\"? Let me think about OIML-CS: certificates reference \"associated reports\" which are the Type Evaluation Reports (TERs). But here the list includes multiple test reports accumulated across revisions, plus the project-number-matching one NMi-4086814-01 (matches project number 4086814). The role vocabulary: type_evaluation_report | test_report | documentation_file | evaluation_report. Given \"The conformity was established by the results of tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\", these are test/evaluation reports. I'll use \"test_report\" for the listed reports. And documentation file T10713-10 as \"documentation_file\".\n\nHmm, actually for the first one \"NMi-15200444-01 ... includes 39 pages\" — original type evaluation report probably. Can't be sure. I'll use \"test_report\" for all listed; that's the literal description (\"tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\"). Fine.\n\nReport dates: parse to ISO:\n- NMi-15200444-01: 2016-03-31, 39 pages\n- NMi-16200309-01 revision 1: 2018-03-22, 29 pages\n- NMi-1902198-01: 2018-12-03, 16 pages\n- NMi-2186668... wait: NMi-2186686-01: 2019-05-24, 43 pages\n- NMi-2186686-02: 2019-05-24, 47 pages\n- NMi-2463352-01: 2021-01-18, 27 pages\n- NMi-3369067-01: 2022-05-10, 13 pages\n- NMi-3670720-01: 2023-09-22, 12 pages\n- NMi-4086814-01: 2026-08-10, 21 pages\n\nDocumentation folder no. T10713-10 → test_reports entry: {\"id\": \"T10713-10\", \"role\": \"documentation_file\"}.\n\nIssuing authority person_title: not given (just \"Person responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\"). The signature block mentions \"Certification Board\" — that might be the title of the signer? The layout: \"Issuing Authority NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1, 10 August 2026, Certification Board\". Hmm. Person responsible is M.Ph.D. Schmidt. I'll leave person_title null/omit. Actually maybe omit.\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"NMi Certin B.V. (OIML Issuing Authority NL1)\" — I'll use name \"NMi Certin B.V.\" and oiml_issuer_id \"NL1\".\n- address_lines: [\"Thijseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"the Netherlands\"]\n- person_responsible: \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\"\n- phone: \"+31 88 636 2332\"\n- email: \"certin@nmi.nl\"\n- website: \"www.nmi.nl\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-19.01\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: \"4086814\"\n- page_total: 7\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- date_issued: \"2026-08-10\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\nWait — this input says \"a fragment of one certificate\" part 1 of 2. It includes pages 1-4 (page 4 mostly empty). So page_total 7 from \"Page 1 of 7\". Good.\n\nRecommendation: id \"R49-1\"? The scheme field in recommendation — \"scheme\": \"A\". accuracy_classes: [1, 2].\n\nHmm — regarding recommendation id canonical: \"R60\" example. For R49 part 1, I'll use \"R49\" as id? The rule says canonical like \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\" — removes \"OIML \" prefix. \"R49-1\" would be the canonical part-specific. Many extractors would keep \"R49-1\". I'll go with \"R49-1\"... Let me decide: The certificate is issued under R49-1 (2013). Precision favors \"R49-1\". I'll add edition 2013.\n\nAccuracy class: \"1 and 2\" → [1, 2].\n\nCharacteristics — water temperature: \"T50 (+0,1 °C / +50 °C)\". Water temp class T50; range 0.1 to 50 °C. Two entries:\n- \"water_temperature_class\": {value: \"T50\"}\n- \"water_temperature_range\": {value: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n\nElectromagnetic environment config entries values: \"E1\", \"E2\" strings.\n\nEnvironmental class: \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\" → string value.\n\nFlow profile sensitivity: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\" — keep the \"x\" as given (avoid × maybe fine either). I'll write \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\".\n\nReverse flow: string.\n\nPressure loss matrix table rows — alternative class N/A → null. Include alternative_q3_limit as string \"Q3 ≤ 25\" with unit m³/h? The condition \"for Q3 ≤ 25 m³/h\". I'll make column \"alternative_q3_limit_m3h\" hmm. Better: columns: meter_size, pressure_loss_class, alternative_pressure_loss_class, alternative_class_condition. rows: {\"meter_size\": \"DN50\", \"pressure_loss_class\": \"Δp 25\", \"alternative_pressure_loss_class\": \"Δp 10\", \"alternative_class_condition\": \"Q3 ≤ 25 m³/h\"}. Keep units inside condition string since it's a condition. Or split into numeric: alternative_q3_max: 25, unit m³/h. Rule 2 applies to \"characteristic value\" objects; matrix table rows are objects per row with raw-ish values. I'll split: \"alternative_q3_max\": 25 (unit column_units \"m³/h\"), null for N/A rows. That's cleaner and machine-readable.\n\ncolumns: [\"meter_size\", \"pressure_loss_class\", \"alternative_pressure_loss_class\", \"alternative_q3_max\"], column_units: [null, null, null, \"m³/h\"].\n\nAlso add description: \"Pressure loss classes for reduced-bore type MUT2300 per documentation 10713/4-03, 10713/12-02 and 10713/12-03; applies to sizes < DN250 (sizes ≥ DN250: Δp 40, i.e. 0.040 MPa or 0.40 bar)\".\n\nSoftware table:\ncolumns: [\"software_type\", \"software_version\", \"crc_checksum\"], rows:\n{\"software_type\": \"Bootloader\", \"software_version\": \"01.00\", \"crc_checksum\": \"63A2EDED\"}, etc. 4 rows (note 01.02 twice).\n\nconfig_level entries list:\n\n1. {\n  \"attribute\": \"electromagnetic_environment\",\n  \"axis\": \"converter_and_configuration\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"MC406M, remote version\", \"value\": \"E1\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"MC406M, compact version\", \"value\": \"E2\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"MC406AM, compact or remote version\", \"value\": \"E2\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"MC406M or MC406AM (compact or remote) with Bluetooth & RS485 and/or 4-20 mA output\", \"value\": \"E1\"}\n  ]\n}\n\n2. {\n  \"attribute\": \"pressure_loss_class\",\n  \"axis\": \"type_and_size\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"full-bore MUT1000EL or MUT2200EL, all sizes (documentation 10713/6-01, 10713/6-02, 10713/12-01)\", \"value\": \"Δp 10\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"reduced-bore MUT2300, size < DN80 (documentation 10713/1-01)\", \"value\": \"Δp 25\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"reduced-bore MUT2300, size ≥ DN80 (documentation 10713/1-01)\", \"value\": \"Δp 40\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"reduced-bore MUT2300, size ≥ DN250 (documentation 10713/4-03, 10713/12-02, 10713/12-03)\", \"value\": \"Δp 40 (0.040 MPa or 0.40 bar)\"}\n  ]\n}\n\nHmm — for value with parenthetical: keep \"Δp 40\" clean and mention conversion in condition? The 0,040 MPa or 0,40 bar is a value detail. I'll keep value \"Δp 40\" and add condition note \"(0.040 MPa or 0.40 bar)\" in condition. Or leave value as \"Δp 40\". Keep simple: value \"Δp 40\".\n\n3. {\n  \"attribute\": \"dc_mains_supply_voltage\",\n  \"axis\": \"converter\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"MC406AM only\", \"value\": {\"min\": 10, \"max\": 28}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"}\n  ]\n}\n\nWait — value shape must be {condition, value, unit_symbol}? Rule 5: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. OK.\n\nmodel_level:\n- accuracy_class: values [{model: \"MUT2200EL\", value: 2}, {model: \"MUT2300\", value: 1}]\n- Also maybe \"bore\" model-level: [{model: MUT1000EL, value: \"full (without flanges)\"}, ...]. I'll put that in model_family attributes instead. Keep model_level for accuracy_class only. Hmm, but pressure loss class per size — sizes DN50..DN200 could be model_level with model ids like \"MUT2300 DN50\". The matrix table covers it. I'll leave matrix table.\n\nActually, rule 4 says for R137-like tables: extract as matrix_tables AND populate model_family.models and model_level. Here the DN sizes are size variants of MUT2300. The models are already MUT1000EL/MUT2200EL/MUT2300. Adding 7 size variants as models would multiply. The family is the type family; sizes are configurations. I think matrix_tables + config_level (axis meter_size) is right. Let me also add config_level for the per-size pressure loss from the table:\n\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"pressure_loss_class_mut2300\",\n  \"axis\": \"meter_size (documentation 10713/4-03, 10713/12-02, 10713/12-03; sizes < DN250)\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"DN50\", \"value\": \"Δp 25\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN65\", \"value\": \"Δp 25\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN80\", \"value\": \"Δp 40\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN100\", \"value\": \"Δp 40\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN125\", \"value\": \"Δp 16\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN150\", \"value\": \"Δp 40\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN200\", \"value\": \"Δp 16\"}\n  ]\n}\n\nThat plus the matrix table covers it. Good.\n\nComponents:\n\n[\n  {\n    \"role\": \"measuring sensor (electromagnetic primary element with cylindrical measuring tube)\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"MUT1000EL\", \"MUT2200EL\", \"MUT2300\"],\n    \"alternatives\": null,\n    \"characteristics\": {\n      \"measuring_tube\": {\n        \"full_bore_variants\": {\"value\": \"MUT1000EL without flanges; MUT2200EL with flanges\"},\n        \"reduced_bore_variant\": {\"value\": \"MUT2300\"}\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"role\": \"electronic converter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"MC406M\", \"MC406AM\"],\n    \"alternatives\": null,\n    \"characteristics\": {\n      \"available_versions\": {\"value\": \"compact and remote\"},\n      \"optional_outputs\": {\"value\": \"Bluetooth & RS485 and/or 4-20 mA output\"}\n    }\n  }\n]\n\nHmm, the nested characteristics format — schema just says characteristics{}. I'll keep it simple with structured values. Actually maybe simpler to have characteristics: {\"versions\": {\"value\": [\"compact\", \"remote\"]}}? Arrays not in shape. Keep strings.\n\nActually, for the measuring sensor, maybe characteristics should be omitted since described elsewhere. I'll include a light one.\n\nmodel_family:\n{\n  \"family_name\": null,\n  \"models\": [\n    {\"model_id\": \"MUT1000EL\", \"label\": \"Full bore measuring tube, without flanges\", \"attributes\": {\"bore\": \"full\", \"flanges\": false}},\n    {\"model_id\": \"MUT2200EL\", \"label\": \"Full bore measuring tube, with flanges\", \"attributes\": {\"bore\": \"full\", \"flanges\": true, \"accuracy_class\": 2, \"pressure_loss_class\": \"Δp 10\"}},\n    {\"model_id\": \"MUT2300\", \"label\": \"Reduced bore measuring tube\", \"attributes\": {\"bore\": \"reduced\", \"accuracy_class\": 1}}\n  ]\n}\n\nmodel_family label — schema shows model_id, label, attributes{}. Good.\n\ncertified_type.description: \"An electromagnetic water meter. The measurement sensor can have a cylindrical measuring tube: full bore for type MUT1000EL (without flanges) or MUT2200EL (with flanges), or reduced bore for type MUT2300; with electronic converter MC406M and MC406AM.\"\n\ntype_designations: [\"MUT1000EL\", \"MUT2200EL\", \"MUT2300\", \"MC406M\", \"MC406AM\"].\n\nmodule_designation: omit (not applicable / not stated). I'll omit.\n\nCategory: \"Water meter\".\n\nNow double-check numbers: MAP 1,6 MPa → 1.6 MPa. Battery 2,9–3,7 V → min 2.9 max 3.7. Water temp +0,1 → 0.1.\n\npage_total: 7.\n\ndate_issued: \"2026-08-10\". The date appears with issuing authority signature: \"10 August 2026\". Also report NMi-4086814-01 dated 10 August 2026 (same project). Good.\n\nrevision_history: [{\"revision\": \"6\", \"date\": \"2026-08-10\"}] — changes omitted (not stated). Should I include? The revision 6 is part of the number. Include for completeness.\n\nTest reports — id strings: \"NMi-15200444-01\", \"NMi-16200309-01\" (revision 1 — note in id? There's no revision field in test_reports schema. I could note in role? no. Add \"NMi-16200309-01 rev. 1\"? Keep id \"NMi-16200309-01\" and maybe append \"(revision 1)\"? I'll keep plain id; revision info not in schema. Hmm, but losing info. The schema: id, date, pages, role. I'll keep id clean.\n\npages as integers.\n\nNow, footnotes: none.\n\nOne more: \"Flow profile sensitivity class\" — value \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\".\n\n\"Reverse flow\": value \"The water meter is designed to measure reverse flow\".\n\n\"Orientation\": value \"All positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\".\n\nEnvironmental class value: \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\".\n\nPower supply type-level: \n- \"power_supply_type\": {\"value\": \"replaceable battery\", ...} hmm the type-level should capture battery; DC mains is config-level. So:\n  type_level: \"battery\": {\"value\": \"replaceable battery\"}, \"battery_voltage_range\": {\"value\": {min:2.9, max:3.7}, \"unit_symbol\":\"V\"}.\n  \nI'll name: \"power_supply_battery\": {\"value\": \"replaceable battery\"} and \"battery_voltage\": {...V}.\n\nOK assemble JSON.\n\nAlso certificate.oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\".\n\nRecommendation scheme \"A\". Include amendment? Not stated → omit.\n\nLet me also consider whether to include \"characteristics\" wrapper with type_level, model_level, config_level keys. Yes.\n\nMatrix table column_units — schema example includes it. Fine to include.\n\nFinal JSON:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-19.01\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"project_number\": \"4086814\",\n    \"page_total\": 7,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2026-08-10\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"NMi Certin B.V. (OIML Issuing Authority NL1)\",\n    \"address_lines\": [\"Thijseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"the Netherlands\"],\n    \"person_responsible\": \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\",\n    \"phone\": \"+31 88 636 2332\",\n    \"email\": \"certin@nmi.nl\",\n    \"website\": \"www.nmi.nl\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"applicants\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Euromag International S.r.l.\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"Via Balla, 55\", \"35010 Villafranca Padovana (PD)\", \"Italy\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"manufacturers\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Euromag International S.r.l.\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"Via Balla, 55\", \"35010 Villafranca Padovana (PD)\", \"Italy\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"MUT1000EL\", \"MUT2200EL\", \"MUT2300\", \"MC406M\", \"MC406AM\"],\n    \"description\": \"An electromagnetic water meter, types MUT1000EL, MUT2200EL and MUT2300, with electronic converter MC406M and MC406AM. The measurement sensor can have a cylindrical measuring tube: full bore for type MUT1000EL (without flanges) or MUT2200EL (with flanges), or reduced bore for type MUT2300.\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": null,\n    \"models\": [\n      {\"model_id\": \"MUT1000EL\", \"label\": \"Full bore, without flanges\", \"attributes\": {\"bore\": \"full\", \"flanges\": false}},\n      {\"model_id\": \"MUT2200EL\", \"label\": \"Full bore, with flanges\", \"attributes\": {\"bore\": \"full\", \"flanges\": true, \"accuracy_class\": 2, \"pressure_loss_class\": \"Δp 10\"}},\n      {\"model_id\": \"MUT2300\", \"label\": \"Reduced bore\", \"attributes\": {\"bore\": \"reduced\", \"accuracy_class\": 1}}\n    ]\n  },\n  \"components\": [...],\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {\n      \"measuring_principle\": {\"value\": \"Electromagnetic\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"environmental_class\": {\"value\": \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"ambient_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -25, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"water_temperature_class\": {\"value\": \"T50\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"water_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": 50}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"maximum_admissible_pressure\": {\"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"MPa\", \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"orientation\": {\"value\": \"All positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\", ...},\n      \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\": {\"value\": \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\", ...},\n      \"reverse_flow\": {\"value\": \"designed to measure reverse flow\", ...},\n      \"power_supply_battery\": {\"value\": \"replaceable battery\", ...},\n      \"battery_voltage\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 2.9, \"max\": 3.7}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\", ...}\n    },\n    \"model_level\": [\n      {\"attribute\": \"accuracy_class\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"values\": [{\"model\": \"MUT2200EL\", \"value\": 2}, {\"model\": \"MUT2300\", \"value\": 1}]}\n    ],\n    \"config_level\": [ ... as above ... ]\n  },\n  \"matrix_tables\": [ pressure loss table, software table ],\n  \"recommendation\": {\"id\": \"R49-1\", \"edition\": 2013, \"scheme\": \"A\", \"accuracy_classes\": [1, 2]},\n  \"test_reports\": [...],\n  \"revision_history\": [{\"revision\": \"6\", \"date\": \"2026-08-10\"}]\n}\n\nShould footnote_markers be included on all values? Rule says structured shape includes footnote_markers; omit when empty. Fine.\n\nFor pressure loss class MUT2300 per-size config, I named attribute \"pressure_loss_class_mut2300\" — attribute labels should be canonical; having type \"MUT2300\" in attribute name is a bit off but conveys. Alternative: axis already says MUT2300. Let me restructure pressure loss into ONE config_level entry with axis covering type/size, plus a second for the per-size table? Or keep two entries: one for the documentation-based statements and one per-size table (which is also documentation 10713/4-03 etc.). The per-size table only applies to sizes < DN250.\n\nConfig entry A (statements):\nattribute: \"pressure_loss_class\", axis: \"type_bore_and_size\", values as above with conditions embedding documentation refs.\n\nConfig entry B (per-size for MUT2300, doc 10713/4-03, 10713/12-02, 10713/12-03, sizes < DN250):\nattribute: \"pressure_loss_class\", axis: \"meter_size (reduced-bore type MUT2300, sizes < DN250)\", values: DN50→Δp25 ... etc.\n\nBoth with attribute \"pressure_loss_class\". Good.\n\nNote: DN125 row: Δp 16 — inconsistent with \"≥DN80 → Δp40\" from other doc, but that's per different documentation; fine, report as-is.\n\nSoftware: include software version \"01.02\" twice. Keep both rows.\n\nFor the matrix table of pressure loss: name \"Pressure loss classes of reduced-bore type MUT2300 for sizes < DN250\"; description: \"Based on documentation 10713/4-03, 10713/12-02 and 10713/12-03. For sizes ≥ DN250: Δp 40 (0.040 MPa or 0.40 bar). Alternative pressure loss class applies under the given Q3 condition.\"\n\nAlso the full-bore statement \"based on documentation 10713/6-01, 10713/6-02 and 10713/12-01\" and reduced-bore Δp25/Δp40 \"based on documentation 10713/1-01\" — in config conditions. Good.\n\nComponents — measuring tube attribute for MUT: keep simple:\n{\"measuring_tube\": {\"value\": \"cylindrical; full bore (MUT1000EL without flanges, MUT2200EL with flanges) or reduced bore (MUT2300)\"}}\n\nConverter component characteristics:\n{\"versions\": {\"value\": \"compact and remote versions\"}, \"optional_interfaces\": {\"value\": \"Bluetooth & RS485 and/or 4-20 mA output\"}}\n\nBoth MC406M and MC406AM. alternatives: null? The relationship: \"electronic converter MC406M and MC406AM\" — both exist in the certificate. alternatives null. Actually could be \"AND\"? The certified types include both converters. Sensor OR relationships: MUT1000EL or MUT2200EL or MUT2300. Hmm, alternatives field for the components entry: when the cert offers alternatives. For sensor: MUT1000EL OR MUT2200EL OR MUT2300 → alternatives \"OR\"? These are alternatives in a sense (each type is a separate model). But they're family members, not alternative components within one instrument. The converter MC406M and MC406AM — alternatives? \"with electronic converter MC406M and MC406AM\" — could pair. I'll set alternatives: null for both, since they're model variants rather than either/or component choices. Hmm, but rule 6: \"When the cert offers alternatives (e.g. 'Transmitter UMC4 OR UMC4-RM'), record alternatives: 'OR'.\" Here \"MUT1000EL, MUT2200EL and MUT2300\" with \"and\" — not OR. Keep null. Omit or null? I'll include null explicitly? Omit to be safe — schema shows the field; I'll include \"alternatives\": null for clarity. Actually omitting is fine. I'll set null.\n\nTest reports final:\n\n[\n  {\"id\": \"NMi-15200444-01\", \"date\": \"2016-03-31\", 